Toward a Formal Theory of Fractal Civilizational Viability

Version 0.4 — Multilayer Viability Kernel and Latency-Constrained Autonomy Matrix

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Kimi 

Abstract

0. Positioning: Fractal Architecture as a Complexity-Management Hypothesis

Tainter’s collapse model holds that civilizations fail when the marginal return on complexity declines. Turchin’s structural-demographic theory adds elite competition and fiscal crisis. This thesis frames fractal civilizational architecture as a testable complexity-management mechanism: if local autonomy and nested modularity can keep coordination costs sublinear in scale while preserving systemic resilience, then the marginal-return curve may be reset.

The formal claim is not that fractal architecture is superior, but that it is a candidate solution to a constrained stochastic viability problem. The formal apparatus below is intended to make that claim falsifiable.

---

1. Multilayer State Space and Notation

Let

\mathcal{J}=\{E,W,F,M,H,I,G,S\}

be the set of essential civilizational functions.

Let V be the set of nodes. For each node i\in V, define the multilayer functional state

s_i(t)=(s_{i,j}(t))_{j\in\mathcal{J}}\in\{0,1\}^{8},

where

s_{i,j}(t)=
\begin{cases}
1, & \text{function } j \text{ is operational at node } i,\\
0, & \text{function } j \text{ is failed at node } i.
\end{cases}

Let x_{i,j}^{\text{baseline}}>0 be the baseline capacity of node i for function j. The effective capacity vector is

x_{i,j}(t)=s_{i,j}(t)x_{i,j}^{\text{baseline}}.

The total capacity for function j is

X_j(t)=\sum_{i\in V}x_{i,j}(t).

Let X_{j,\min}>0. The viability region is

\mathcal{V}
=
\left\{
X\in\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^{8}: X_j\ge X_{j,\min}\ \forall j\in\mathcal{J}
\right\}.

The civilization is viable at time t if X(t)\in\mathcal{V}.

Remark 1.1 — Why multilayer instead of single-layer
A single-layer model assumes that failure of any function at a node collapses all other functions at that node. This is strong interdependence. A multilayer model with tolerance parameters allows energy failure to degrade life support gradually or not at all, depending on buffers. This changes the percolation transition from abrupt to mixed, and is necessary for realistic viability analysis.

