2. Stochastic Viability and the Resilience Functional

2.1 Exogenous Disturbance Process

For each node-function pair (i,j), exogenous failure events arrive as an independent Poisson process with rate \lambda_{i,j}>0. At each failure event, s_{i,j} jumps from 1 to 0. Repair is also exponential: s_{i,j} returns from 0 to 1 at rate \eta_{i,j}>0.

Endogenous propagation is defined by the multilayer cascade process in Section 5.

2.2 Resilience Functional

Let \theta denote the architectural parameters. The resilience functional is

R_T(\theta)
=
\mathbb{P}\left[
X(t)\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t\in[0,T]
\;\middle|\;
\theta,\ X(0)=X_0
\right],

where X_0\in\mathcal{V}, and the probability is taken over exogenous failures, repairs, and endogenous cascades.

2.3 Stochastic Viability Kernel

The resilience functional is only meaningful if the initial state is not already doomed.

Define the maximal viability probability

V^*(X_0)
=
\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}
\mathbb{P}_{X_0}^{u}
\left[
X(t)\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t\in[0,T]
\right],

where u ranges over admissible control policies, including active boundary isolation and resource reallocation.

Definition 2.1 — Stochastic viability kernel

For tolerance \varepsilon\in[0,1),

\operatorname{Viab}^{\varepsilon}(\mathcal{V})
=
\left\{
X_0\in\mathcal{V}
:
V^*(X_0)\ge 1-\varepsilon
\right\}.

Proposition 2.2 — Feasibility bound

For any fixed architecture \theta with induced control policy u_\theta,

R_T(\theta)
\le
V^*(X_0).

In particular, if

X_0\notin \operatorname{Viab}^{\varepsilon}(\mathcal{V}),

then

R_T(\theta)<1-\varepsilon.

Proof.
The architecture \theta chooses one admissible policy u_\theta. Hence

R_T(\theta)
=
\mathbb{P}_{X_0}^{u_\theta}
\left[
X(t)\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t
\right]
\le
\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}
\mathbb{P}_{X_0}^{u}
\left[
X(t)\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t
\right]
=
V^*(X_0).

If X_0\notin \operatorname{Viab}^{\varepsilon}(\mathcal{V}), then by definition V^*(X_0)<1-\varepsilon, so R_T(\theta)<1-\varepsilon. \square

Consequence.
Proposition 2.2 converts the optimization problem from a mere existence claim into a feasibility-constrained problem. An architecture can be optimal only if it operates from a state inside the viability kernel.

