3. Latency Constraints and Local Autonomy

3.1 Round-Trip Delay Stability

Centralized continuous feedback over Earth–Mars distance involves a sensor measurement traveling from Mars to Earth and an actuator command traveling back. The relevant loop delay is therefore

\tau_{\text{loop}}=2\tau_{\text{one-way}}.

Consider a time-critical scalar control loop:

\dot{x}(t)=-k x(t-\tau_{\text{loop}}),
\qquad k>0.

Theorem 3.1 — Latency stability bound

The zero solution of

\dot{x}(t)=-k x(t-\tau_{\text{loop}})

is asymptotically stable if and only if

k\tau_{\text{loop}}<\frac{\pi}{2}.

Proof.
Substitute x(t)=e^{\lambda t}. The characteristic equation is

\lambda + k e^{-\lambda \tau_{\text{loop}}}=0.

At \tau_{\text{loop}}=0, the unique root is \lambda=-k<0, so the system is stable.

The roots depend continuously on \tau_{\text{loop}}. Stability can be lost only if a root crosses the imaginary axis. Set \lambda=i\omega, \omega>0. Then

i\omega + k e^{-i\omega \tau_{\text{loop}}}=0.

Taking magnitudes gives

k=\omega.

Therefore

e^{-i k \tau_{\text{loop}}}=-i=e^{-i\pi/2}.

The first positive boundary is

k\tau_{\text{loop}}=\frac{\pi}{2}.

It remains to verify that the root crosses from left to right. Let

\Delta(\lambda,\tau)=\lambda + k e^{-\lambda \tau}.

Implicit differentiation gives

\Delta_\lambda \lambda'+\Delta_\tau=0,

where

\Delta_\lambda=1-k\tau e^{-\lambda\tau},
\qquad
\Delta_\tau=-k\lambda e^{-\lambda\tau}.

At \lambda=ik and \tau=\pi/(2k),

e^{-\lambda\tau}=-i,

so

\Delta_\lambda=1+\frac{i\pi}{2},
\qquad
\Delta_\tau=-k^2.

Thus

\lambda'
=
-\frac{\Delta_\tau}{\Delta_\lambda}
=
\frac{k^2}{1+\frac{i\pi}{2}}
=
\frac{k^2\left(1-\frac{i\pi}{2}\right)}{1+\frac{\pi^2}{4}}.

Hence

\operatorname{Re}(\lambda')
=
\frac{k^2}{1+\frac{\pi^2}{4}}
>
0.

The root crosses from the left half-plane to the right half-plane at \tau=\pi/(2k). Therefore the system is stable if and only if

k\tau_{\text{loop}}<\frac{\pi}{2}.

\square

3.2 Network Consensus and Delayed Governance

Governance is not a scalar control loop. A minimal model is a delayed consensus protocol on a communication graph.

Let L\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n} be a symmetric positive definite graph Laplacian, and consider

\dot{x}(t)=-L x(t-\tau_{\text{loop}}).

Let \lambda_{\max}(L)>0 be the largest eigenvalue of L.

Lemma 3.2 — Network delay stability

The zero solution of

\dot{x}(t)=-L x(t-\tau_{\text{loop}})

is asymptotically stable if and only if

\tau_{\text{loop}}\lambda_{\max}(L)<\frac{\pi}{2}.

Proof.
Since L is symmetric, diagonalize L=U\Lambda U^\top. Define y(t)=U^\top x(t). The system decouples into scalar modes:

\dot y_j(t)=-\lambda_j y_j(t-\tau_{\text{loop}}).

By Theorem 3.1, mode j is stable if and only if

\lambda_j \tau_{\text{loop}}<\frac{\pi}{2}.

The strictest condition is imposed by \lambda_{\max}(L). \square

Definition 3.3 — Function-specific required local autonomy

For function j, let \tau_{\text{decision},j} be the characteristic decision timescale of its control loop. Define

a_{i,j}^{\min}
=
\begin{cases}
1, & \tau_{\text{decision},j}<\frac{2}{\pi}\tau_{\text{loop}},\\[4pt]
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Equivalently,

a_{i,j}^{\min}=1
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\tau_{\text{loop}}>\frac{\pi}{2}\tau_{\text{decision},j}.

Corollary 3.4 — Earth–Mars autonomy floor

For Earth–Mars,

\tau_{\text{loop}}\in[480,2880]\ \text{seconds}.

Life-support, power stability, thermal control, and emergency response have \tau_{\text{decision}}<1 minute, so

a_{i,j}^{\min}=1

for those functions. Long-term economic planning has \tau_{\text{decision}}>1 hour, so

a_{i,j}^{\min}=0.

The autonomy floor is therefore function-specific, not a scalar preference.

