4. Autonomy Matrix and Coordination Tradeoff

4.1 Autonomy Matrix

Let

A=(a_{i,j})\in[0,1]^{|V|\times 8}

be the autonomy matrix, where a_{i,j} is the fraction of control decisions for function j at node i made locally.

Define the set of latency-critical functions:

\mathcal{J}_c
=
\left\{
j\in\mathcal{J}
:
a_{i,j}^{\min}=1
\text{ for all } i\in V
\right\}.

For j\in\mathcal{J}_c, we impose

a_{i,j}=1.

For non-critical functions j\notin\mathcal{J}_c, define

u_j\in[0,1]

and set

a_{i,j}=u_j.

Thus the autonomy matrix is parameterized by

\mathbf{u}=(u_j)_{j\notin\mathcal{J}_c}.

The aggregate local autonomy is

\bar A(\mathbf{u})
=
\frac{1}{N|\mathcal{J}|}
\sum_{i,j}a_{i,j}(\mathbf{u})
=
\frac{|\mathcal{J}_c|}{|\mathcal{J}|}
+
\frac{1}{|\mathcal{J}|}
\sum_{j\notin\mathcal{J}_c}u_j.

4.2 Coordination Loss and Isolation Benefit

Assumption 4.1 — Coordination loss

Let

K:[0,1]\to[0,1]

be twice continuously differentiable and strictly decreasing, with

K(0)=1.

Define duplication/coordination loss

D(\bar A)=1-K(\bar A).

Then

D'(\bar A)=-K'(\bar A)>0.

Assumption 4.2 — Autonomy–isolation mapping

Let

\Phi:[0,1]\to[0,1]

be twice continuously differentiable, strictly increasing, and concave, with

\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi(1)=1.

Here \Phi(\bar A) is the probability that an inter-module edge carrying failure can be severed.

Definition 4.3 — Autonomy benefit from cascade reduction

Let R_0(\bar A) be the spectral radius of the multilayer next-generation matrix defined in Section 5. Define the benefit function

B(\bar A)
=
B_0+\beta \Phi(\bar A),

where B_0=1-\rho_{\text{in}}c_{\text{in}}-\rho_{\text{out}}^0 c_{\text{out}} and

\beta=\rho_{\text{out}}^0 c_{\text{out}}>0.

Then B(\bar A) is concave and increasing.

Define the architectural score

V(\bar A)
=
B(\bar A)-\kappa D(\bar A),

where \kappa>0.

Proposition 4.4 — Existence of interior optimal autonomy

Let

\bar A_{\min}=\frac{|\mathcal{J}_c|}{|\mathcal{J}|},
\qquad
\bar A_{\max}=1.

Suppose

V'(\bar A_{\min})>0,
\qquad
V'(\bar A_{\max})<0.

Then there exists at least one interior \bar A^*\in(\bar A_{\min},\bar A_{\max}) maximizing V.

If, in addition,

V''(\bar A)<0

for all \bar A\in[\bar A_{\min},\bar A_{\max}], then \bar A^* is unique.

Proof.
By the intermediate value theorem applied to V', there exists \bar A^* such that

V'(\bar A^*)=0.

Since V is continuous on the compact interval [\bar A_{\min},\bar A_{\max}], it attains a maximum. If V''<0, then V' is strictly decreasing, so the critical point is unique. \square

Remark 4.5
The boundary conditions are now expressed in terms of cascade parameters and the latency-imposed autonomy floor. The theory does not predict an interior optimum by default. If V'(\bar A_{\min})\le 0, maximum centralization of non-critical functions is optimal. If V'(\bar A_{\max})\ge 0, maximum decentralization is optimal.

