5. Multilayer Cascade Process with Weak Interdependence

5.1 Multilayer Network and Tolerance Matrix

Partition V into m modules V_1,\dots,V_m. For each module, let p_{\text{in}} be the probability of an intra-module edge and p_{\text{out}} the probability of an inter-module edge. Define graph modularity

\mathcal{M}
=
1-\frac{p_{\text{out}}}{p_{\text{in}}}

for p_{\text{in}}>0.

Let

\alpha=(\alpha_{j,j'})_{j,j'\in\mathcal{J}}

be a tolerance matrix, where \alpha_{j,j'}\in[0,1] is the degree to which function j is buffered against the failure of function j'. By convention,

\alpha_{j,j}=0.

The effective coupling from j' to j is

1-\alpha_{j,j'}.

5.2 Node-Level Markov Cascade Process

Let

s(t)=(s_{i,j}(t))_{i\in V,\ j\in\mathcal{J}}

be the full state.

For each node i and function j, the transition rates are as follows.

Exogenous failure:

s_{i,j}:1\to 0
\quad\text{at rate }
\lambda_{i,j}.

Repair:

s_{i,j}:0\to 1
\quad\text{at rate }
\eta_{i,j}.

Endogenous propagation:

If s_{i,j}=1, then it fails at rate

\Lambda_{i,j}(s)
=
\sum_{j'\neq j}
\rho^{\text{node}}_{j,j'}
(1-\alpha_{j,j'})
\mathbf{1}_{s_{i,j'}=0}
+
\sum_{k\neq i}\sum_{j'\in\mathcal{J}}
\mathcal{R}_{(i,j),(k,j')}(A)
\mathbf{1}_{s_{k,j'}=0},

where

\mathcal{R}_{(i,j),(k,j')}(A)
=
\begin{cases}
\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{in}}
(1-\alpha_{j,j'}),
& i,k \text{ same module},\\[4pt]
\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{out},0}
(1-\alpha_{j,j'})
(1-\Phi_j(A)),
& i,k \text{ different module}.
\end{cases}

Here \Phi_j(A) is the probability that an inter-module edge carrying failure into function j is severed. It depends on the local autonomy of the source module for function j.

Definition 5.1 — Active boundary isolation

If the fraction of failed nodes in a module exceeds a detection threshold \sigma, then with probability \Phi_j(A), all inter-module edges carrying failure into function j are severed. Severed edges transmit no further failure.

5.3 Mean-Field Next-Generation Matrix

Define the expected internal degree c_{\text{in}} and external degree c_{\text{out}}. Define the next-generation matrix M(A)\in\mathbb{R}^{8\times 8} by

M_{j,j'}(A)
=
\frac{1}{\eta_{j'}}
\left[
\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{node}}(1-\alpha_{j,j'})
+
c_{\text{in}}\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{in}}(1-\alpha_{j,j'})
+
c_{\text{out}}\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{out},0}(1-\alpha_{j,j'})(1-\Phi_j(A))
\right].

The mean-field reproduction number is the spectral radius:

R_0(A)=\rho(M(A)).

Proposition 5.2 — Isolation reduces the reproduction number

Let \Phi(A)\le \tilde\Phi(A) entrywise. Then

R_0(\Phi(A))\ge R_0(\tilde\Phi(A)).

Proof.
Increasing \Phi_j(A) decreases the term

1-\Phi_j(A),

so M(A) is entrywise non-increasing. By Perron–Frobenius theory, the spectral radius of a nonnegative matrix is monotone nondecreasing in its entries. \square

Proposition 5.3 — Subcriticality under isolation

Suppose

R_0(0)<1.

Then the mean-field branching process is subcritical even without isolation. If

R_0(0)>1

but the internal-only spectral radius satisfies

\rho\left(
\left[
\frac{1}{\eta_{j'}}
\left(
\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{node}}(1-\alpha_{j,j'})
+
c_{\text{in}}\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{in}}(1-\alpha_{j,j'})
\right)
\right]_{j,j'}
\right)
<
1,

then there exists an isolation level \Phi^*<1 such that

R_0(\Phi^*)<1.

Proof.
The first statement is immediate. For the second, note that at full isolation \Phi_j=1, the external terms vanish. The remaining internal matrix has spectral radius less than 1. By continuity, there exists \Phi^*<1 such that R_0(\Phi^*)<1. \square

Proposition 5.4 — Modularity is not monotone in resilience

There exist stochastic block networks G_1,G_2,G_3 such that

\mathcal{M}(G_1)<\mathcal{M}(G_2)
\quad\text{but}\quad
R_0(G_1)<R_0(G_2),

and

\mathcal{M}(G_3)<\mathcal{M}(G_2)
\quad\text{but}\quad
R_0(G_3)>R_0(G_2).

Proof.
Use the explicit construction from v0.3, replacing \mu by R_0. The argument is identical and is omitted here. \square

Remark 5.5
The mean-field next-generation matrix is an approximation. The node-level Markov process is the true object. Future work should derive explicit approximation bounds using coupling methods or branching-process limit theorems.

