6. Fractal Architecture and Governance Latency Hierarchy

6.1 Nested Modular Architecture

Let the civilization be organized into L scale levels, l=0,1,\dots,L.

· Level 0: individual nodes.
· Level l\ge 1: each level-l-1 group belongs to exactly one level-l group.

Definition 6.1 — Layer-specific functional fractal property

A nested modular architecture satisfies the functional fractal property if, for every latency-critical function j\in\mathcal{J}_c, for every level l\ge 1, and for every group g at level l,

\sum_{i\in g} x_{i,j}
\ge
x_{j,\min}^{(l)},

where x_{j,\min}^{(l)} is a scale-dependent local minimum for function j.

For non-latency-critical functions, local sufficiency at every scale is not required.

6.2 Governance as Delayed Hierarchical Consensus

Let G be the governance layer. At level l, a decision is made by a quorum of functional governance nodes in a group. Let L^{(l)} be the communication graph Laplacian among governance nodes at level l. Let \tau_{\text{loop}}^{(l)} be the round-trip communication delay within that group.

Lemma 6.2 — Governance viability condition

Governance consensus at level l is asymptotically stable only if

\tau_{\text{loop}}^{(l)}
\lambda_{\max}(L^{(l)})
<
\frac{\pi}{2}.

Proof.
This is Lemma 3.2 applied to L^{(l)}. \square

Definition 6.3 — Governance latency index

Define

\Gamma_l
=
\frac{2}{\pi}
\tau_{\text{loop}}^{(l)}
\lambda_{\max}(L^{(l)}).

If \Gamma_l\ge 1, governance at level l cannot be stabilized by continuous consensus. Decisions must either be pre-authorized or devolved to a lower scale.

Consequence.
The functional fractal property for governance is not stipulated; it follows from Lemma 6.2. If \Gamma_l\ge 1 at scale l, then G must be locally autonomous at scale l-1.

