7. Optimization Problem with Viability Kernel Feasibility

Let

\theta=(\mathbf{u},Q),

where \mathbf{u} is the non-critical autonomy vector and Q\ge 0 is a redundancy parameter.

Define energy and mass costs:

E(\theta)=\sum_{i\in V}e_i(A(\mathbf{u}),Q),
\qquad
M_{\text{res}}(\theta)=\sum_{i\in V}m_i(A(\mathbf{u}),Q).

Definition 7.1 — Formal optimization problem

\max_{\theta}
R_T(\theta)

subject to

E(\theta)\le E_{\max},
\qquad
M_{\text{res}}(\theta)\le M_{\max},
\qquad
K(\bar A(\mathbf{u}))\ge K_{\min},

and

X_0\in \operatorname{Viab}^{\varepsilon}(\mathcal{V}).

Proposition 7.2 — Existence of optimum under continuity

If the feasible set \Theta is compact and R_T:\Theta\to[0,1] is continuous, then there exists \theta^*\in\Theta maximizing R_T.

Proof.
Weierstrass extreme value theorem. \square

Remark 7.3 — Continuity is strong
In finite-sample simulation, R_T is estimated from Monte Carlo and may be discontinuous due to threshold effects. Future work should replace continuity with a stochastic dominance or Lipschitz approximation.

