8. Falsification Protocol and Pre-Registered Architecture

The central hypothesis is pre-registered as a null hypothesis.

Let

H_0:\quad R_{\text{fractal}}\le R_{\text{central}},
\qquad
H_1:\quad R_{\text{fractal}}>R_{\text{central}}.

8.1 Pre-Registered Scenarios

Five scenarios are fixed before simulation:

1. S1: Earth–Moon, low disturbance rate, baseline latency.
2. S2: Earth–Moon, high disturbance rate, intermittent link outage.
3. S3: Earth–Mars, low disturbance rate, one-way latency 240 s.
4. S4: Earth–Mars, high disturbance rate, one-way latency 1440 s.
5. S5: Earth–Mars, conjunction-level latency 1440 s, with additional supply disruption.

8.2 Simulation Parameters

These are future simulation parameters, not empirical measurements.

Parameter Value
Number of nodes N 100
Modules 2 (Earth module 50, Mars module 50)
Intra-module edge probability p_{\text{in}} 0.20
Inter-module edge probability p_{\text{out}} 0.02
Exogenous failure rate \lambda 0.001 per function per hour
Repair rate \eta 0.1 per hour
Internal propagation rate \rho_{\text{in}} 0.05 per hour
External propagation rate \rho_{\text{out}}^0 0.10 per hour
Tolerance \alpha_{E,H} 0.3
Tolerance \alpha_{H,G} 0.5
Detection threshold \sigma 0.15
One-way latency S3/S4/S5 240 s / 1440 s / 1440 s
Decision time, life support 10 s
Decision time, governance 3600 s
Simulation horizon T 30 days
Monte Carlo replications 10,000 per scenario
Bootstrap method Studentized block bootstrap
Random seed protocol Pre-registered SHA-256 seed

8.3 Statistical Decision Rule

For each scenario s, compute

\Delta_s
=
R_{\text{fractal}}^{(s)}
-
R_{\text{central}}^{(s)}.

The global null hypothesis is rejected if:

1. Primary criterion: at least 4 of 5 scenario-specific one-sided 95% bootstrap confidence intervals for \Delta_s have lower bound greater than 0; and
2. Secondary criterion: the lower bound of a one-sided 95% confidence interval for a random-effects pooled mean of \Delta_s is greater than 0.

8.4 Falsification Conditions

The formal theory is weakened if any of the following hold in a majority of scenarios:

· increasing local autonomy systematically reduces R_T;
· modular architectures have lower R_T than centralized alternatives;
· duplication costs dominate resilience benefits;
· coordination failures systematically outweigh fault-containment benefits;
· no stable interior autonomy allocation exists.

This protocol is intended for future computational evaluation only. No simulations are run in this draft.

