10. Limitations and Formal Open Problems

· The next-generation matrix M(A) is mean-field. Error bounds remain open.
· The autonomy–coordination mapping K(\bar A) and \Phi(\bar A) are assumed smooth. Real systems may exhibit thresholds.
· Resource functions e_i,m_i are generic. Explicit engineering forms are needed for simulation.
· The viability kernel is defined but not computed here.
· Governance is modeled only as consensus dynamics. Strategic behavior, coalition formation, and decision failure remain open.
· No empirical claim is made. All results are conditional.

---

11. Conclusion

Version 0.4 replaces the scalar, single-layer formalism with a multilayer viability framework. The physical latency bound is now imposed as a hard constraint on a function-specific autonomy matrix. The stochastic viability kernel ensures that the optimization problem is feasible, not merely formally solvable. Governance is tied to the same latency stability condition through delayed consensus. The falsification protocol is explicit and pre-registered.

The next step is not another conceptual expansion. It is the construction of the multilayer viability kernel computation and the pre-registered simulator. This document supplies the formal and architectural basis for that step.

