Appendix B. Stochastic Viability Kernel: Dynamic Programming and Feasibility

B.1 Controlled Markov Jump Process

The multilayer cascade process is a continuous-time Markov chain on the finite state space \mathcal{S}. For each state x\in\mathcal{S} and control u\in\mathcal{U}, let q_{x,y}(u)\ge 0 denote the transition rate from x to y\neq x. Define the total exit rate

q_x(u)=\sum_{y\neq x}q_{x,y}(u).

The process evolves as follows: if at time t the state is x and control u is selected, the next jump time is exponentially distributed with rate q_x(u), and the next state is y with probability q_{x,y}(u)/q_x(u).

B.2 Viability Probability and Value Function

For a fixed feedback policy u_\theta, define

V^{u_\theta}(x)
=
\mathbb{P}_x^{u_\theta}\left[
X(t)\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t\in[0,T]
\right].

For infinite-horizon viability, set

V^*(x)
=
\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}
\mathbb{P}_x^{u}\left[
X(t)\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t\ge 0
\right].

We note that T-horizon and infinite-horizon formulations are equivalent for finite Markov chains with absorbing boundary; we use infinite horizon for simplicity.

B.3 Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman Equation on the Viability Kernel

Let K=\mathcal{V}\cap\mathcal{S}. Define the exit set \partial K=\{x\in\mathcal{S}: x\notin \mathcal{V}\}. For x\in K, define the generator of the killed process under control u:

(A^u f)(x)
=
\sum_{y\in K,\,y\neq x} q_{x,y}(u)\bigl(f(y)-f(x)\bigr)
-
\sum_{y\in\partial K} q_{x,y}(u) f(x).

Equivalently,

(A^u f)(x)
=
\sum_{y\in\mathcal{S}} q_{x,y}(u)\bigl(\tilde f(y)-\tilde f(x)\bigr),

where \tilde f(y)=f(y) for y\in K and \tilde f(y)=0 for y\notin K.

Proposition B.1 — HJB equation for viability probability

The value function V^* is the unique bounded solution of

\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}
(A^u V)(x)
=
0
\qquad
\forall x\in K,

with boundary condition

V(x)=0
\qquad
\forall x\in\partial K,

and

0\le V(x)\le 1.

Proof.
We use uniformization. Define

\Lambda
=
\sup_{x\in\mathcal{S},\,u\in\mathcal{U}}
q_x(u),

which is finite by Assumption A3. For each x\in\mathcal{S}, define the uniformized transition probabilities

P_{x,y}(u)
=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{q_{x,y}(u)}{\Lambda}, & y\neq x,\\[6pt]
1-\dfrac{q_x(u)}{\Lambda}, & y=x.
\end{cases}

Then the continuous-time chain is equivalent to a discrete-time Markov chain with transition matrix P(u) where jumps occur at rate \Lambda, and between jumps the state remains unchanged.

Let P_{K}(u) be the submatrix of P(u) restricted to K, including self-loops for x\in K when the process remains in K. The probability of never leaving K starting from x\in K under a fixed policy u is the absorption probability of the chain with transitions P_K(u) into the absorbing exterior. The maximal absorption probability V satisfies the dynamic programming equation:

V(x)
=
\max_{u\in\mathcal{U}}
\left[
\sum_{y\in K} P_{x,y}(u) V(y)
\right]
\qquad
\forall x\in K.

Now substitute P_{x,y}(u)=q_{x,y}(u)/\Lambda for y\neq x and P_{x,x}(u)=1-q_x(u)/\Lambda. Then

V(x)
=
\max_{u}\left[
\sum_{y\in K,\,y\neq x} \frac{q_{x,y}(u)}{\Lambda} V(y)
+
\left(1-\frac{q_x(u)}{\Lambda}\right)V(x)
\right].

Rearranging,

0
=
\max_{u}\left[
\sum_{y\in K,\,y\neq x} q_{x,y}(u)(V(y)-V(x))
-
\sum_{y\in\partial K} q_{x,y}(u) V(x)
\right],

which is exactly

\sup_{u}(A^u V)(x)=0.

Uniqueness follows from the contraction mapping principle on the uniformized chain: the operator T defined by

(TV)(x)
=
\max_{u\in\mathcal{U}}
\left[
\sum_{y\in K} P_{x,y}(u) V(y)
\right]

is a contraction in the sup norm on bounded functions, and V is its unique fixed point in the cone [0,1]. \square

Definition B.2 — Stochastic viability kernel

For \varepsilon\in[0,1),

\operatorname{Viab}^{\varepsilon}(\mathcal{V})
=
\left\{
x\in\mathcal{V}\cap\mathcal{S}
:
V^*(x)\ge 1-\varepsilon
\right\}.

Proposition B.3 — Feasibility bound

For any architecture \theta with induced control policy u_\theta,

R_T(\theta)
\le
V^*(X(0)).

In particular, if

X(0)\notin \operatorname{Viab}^{\varepsilon}(\mathcal{V}),

then

R_T(\theta)<1-\varepsilon.

Proof.
The architecture selects a particular policy u_\theta, so

R_T(\theta)
=
\mathbb{P}_{X(0)}^{u_\theta}
\left[
X(t)\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t\in[0,T]
\right]
\le
\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}
\mathbb{P}_{X(0)}^{u}
\left[
X(t)\in\mathcal{V}\ \forall t\ge 0
\right]
=
V^*(X(0)).

The rest follows from the definition. \square

