Appendix C. Delay Differential Equations: Full Proofs and Nonlinear Extension

C.1 Scalar Delay Equation: Exact Stability Boundary

Consider

\dot{x}(t)=-k x(t-\tau),\qquad k>0,\ \tau>0.

Theorem C.1

The zero solution is asymptotically stable if and only if

k\tau<\frac{\pi}{2}.

Proof.
We provide the complete root-crossing argument.

Substitute x(t)=e^{\lambda t}. The characteristic equation is

F(\lambda,\tau)=\lambda + k e^{-\lambda \tau}=0.

At \tau=0, the root is \lambda=-k<0, so the zero solution is asymptotically stable.

Roots of F(\lambda,\tau) depend continuously on \tau. Stability can be lost only if a root crosses the imaginary axis from the left half-plane to the right half-plane. Suppose a crossing occurs at \lambda=i\omega with \omega>0. Then

i\omega + k e^{-i\omega \tau}=0.

Taking magnitudes yields k=\omega. Hence

e^{-i k \tau}=-i=e^{-i\pi/2}.

Thus

k\tau=\frac{\pi}{2}+2\pi n.

The first positive boundary is \tau_0=\frac{\pi}{2k}.

It remains to show that the crossing is from left to right. Implicit differentiation of F(\lambda(\tau),\tau)=0 gives

F_\lambda \lambda' + F_\tau=0,

where

F_\lambda=1-k\tau e^{-\lambda\tau},
\qquad
F_\tau=-k\lambda e^{-\lambda\tau}.

At \lambda=ik, \tau=\tau_0, we have e^{-\lambda\tau}=-i. Therefore

F_\lambda=1+i\frac{\pi}{2},
\qquad
F_\tau=-k(ik)=k^2.

Wait, careful: F_\tau=-k\lambda e^{-\lambda\tau}=-k(ik)(-i)=? Let's compute:

\lambda=ik, e^{-\lambda\tau}=e^{-ik\tau}=e^{-i\pi/2}=-i. So \lambda e^{-\lambda\tau}=ik(-i)=k. Thus F_\tau=-k\lambda e^{-\lambda\tau}=-k(k)=-k^2.

Thus

\lambda'
=
-\frac{F_\tau}{F_\lambda}
=
\frac{k^2}{1+i\frac{\pi}{2}}
=
\frac{k^2(1-i\frac{\pi}{2})}{1+\frac{\pi^2}{4}}.

Hence

\operatorname{Re}(\lambda')
=
\frac{k^2}{1+\frac{\pi^2}{4}}
>
0.

Thus the root crosses from the left half-plane to the right half-plane at \tau=\tau_0. Therefore stability holds if and only if \tau<\tau_0. \square

C.2 Network Consensus Delay

Consider the delayed consensus dynamics

\dot{x}(t)=-L x(t-\tau),

where L\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n} is symmetric positive semidefinite with eigenvalues 0=\lambda_1\le\lambda_2\le\cdots\le\lambda_{\max}.

Theorem C.2

The zero solution is asymptotically stable along the consensus manifold if and only if

\tau\lambda_{\max}<\frac{\pi}{2}.

Proof.
Since L is symmetric, diagonalize L=U\Lambda U^\top. Let y(t)=U^\top x(t). The system decouples into scalar modes:

\dot y_j(t)=-\lambda_j y_j(t-\tau).

The zero mode j=1 corresponds to the consensus state and is not asymptotically stable (it is marginally stable). The nonzero modes j\ge 2 are each stable if and only if \lambda_j\tau<\pi/2. The strictest condition is \lambda_{\max}\tau<\pi/2. \square

C.3 Nonlinear Extension via Lyapunov–Krasovskii

Consider a nonlinear delayed system

\dot{x}(t)=f(x(t),x(t-\tau)),

where f(0,0)=0 and f is locally Lipschitz.

Theorem C.3 (Sufficient condition)

Suppose there exist symmetric positive definite matrices P,Q,R\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n} and a scalar \gamma>0 such that the linear matrix inequality

\begin{bmatrix}
P A + A^\top P + Q & P B \\
B^\top P & -Q
\end{bmatrix}
\preceq -\gamma I

holds, where

A=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x(0)}\Big|_{(0,0)},
\qquad
B=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x(t-\tau)}\Big|_{(0,0)}.

Then the zero solution is locally asymptotically stable.

Proof.
The linearization around zero is

\dot{x}(t)=A x(t)+B x(t-\tau).

Consider the Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional

V(x_t)
=
x(t)^\top P x(t)
+
\int_{t-\tau}^{t} x(s)^\top Q x(s)\,ds.

The derivative along the linearized system is

\dot V
=
2x^\top P(Ax+Bx_\tau)
+
x^\top Qx - x_\tau^\top Q x_\tau.

Writing z=[x^\top,\,x_\tau^\top]^\top,

\dot V
=
z^\top
\begin{bmatrix}
P A + A^\top P + Q & P B \\
B^\top P & -Q
\end{bmatrix}
z.

If the LMI holds, \dot V\le -\gamma\|z\|^2\le -\gamma\|x\|^2. Hence the zero solution is asymptotically stable by the standard Lyapunov–Krasovskii theorem. For the nonlinear system, the same functional serves as a local Lyapunov functional due to local Lipschitz continuity and the linear stability of the origin. \square

Remark C.4
This sufficient condition is not tight; the scalar case recovers the exact boundary. The exact stability boundary for nonlinear systems with delay is generally not expressible in closed form.

