Appendix D. Multilayer Cascade Process: Construction and Mean-Field Limit

D.1 State Space and Transition Rates

The full state is

s=(s_{i,j})_{i\in V,\,j\in\mathcal{J}}
\in
\mathcal{S}=\{0,1\}^{N|\mathcal{J}|}.

For each node i and function j, the transition rates are:

Exogenous failure:

s_{i,j}:1\to 0 \quad\text{at rate}\quad \lambda_{i,j}.

Repair:

s_{i,j}:0\to 1 \quad\text{at rate}\quad \eta_{i,j}.

Endogenous propagation:

If s_{i,j}=1, then it fails at rate

\Lambda_{i,j}(s)
=
\sum_{j'\neq j}
\rho^{\text{node}}_{j,j'}
(1-\alpha_{j,j'})
\mathbf{1}_{s_{i,j'}=0}
+
\sum_{k\neq i}\sum_{j'\in\mathcal{J}}
\mathcal{R}_{(i,j),(k,j')}(A)
\mathbf{1}_{s_{k,j'}=0},

where

\mathcal{R}_{(i,j),(k,j')}(A)
=
\begin{cases}
\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{in}}
(1-\alpha_{j,j'}),
& i,k \text{ same module},\\[6pt]
\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{out},0}
(1-\alpha_{j,j'})
(1-\Phi_j(A)),
& i,k \text{ different module}.
\end{cases}

Active boundary isolation:

If the fraction of failed nodes in a module exceeds a threshold \sigma, then with probability \Phi_j(A), all inter-module edges carrying failure into function j are severed. In the Markov model, this is implemented by modifying the rates \rho_{j,j'}^{\text{out}} to

\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{out}}
=
\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{out},0}
(1-\Phi_j(A)),

and setting the severed edges to zero transmission permanently for the duration of the isolation. If isolation is reversible, a recovery rate for edges can be added.

D.2 Well-Posedness

Proposition D.1

The continuous-time Markov chain on the finite state space \mathcal{S} with transition rates as defined is well-posed and nonexplosive.

Proof.
The state space is finite, and all transition rates are bounded by Assumption A3. Hence the generator is a finite |\mathcal{S}|\times|\mathcal{S}| matrix with nonnegative off-diagonal entries and nonpositive diagonal. The associated semigroup exists and is unique. \square

D.3 Mean-Field Next-Generation Matrix

We derive the next-generation matrix M(A) from the branching process approximation.

Consider a single failed node i with function j' in a fully functional background. We count the expected number of secondary failures it causes before repair.

Infection rates. The rate at which node i in state s_{i,j'}=0 infects a neighboring functional node k in function j is \rho_{j,j'}^{\text{in}}(1-\alpha_{j,j'}) if i,k are in the same module, and \rho_{j,j'}^{\text{out},0}(1-\alpha_{j,j'})(1-\Phi_j(A)) if they are in different modules. Let c_{\text{in}} be the expected number of intra-module neighbors and c_{\text{out}} the expected number of inter-module neighbors.

Duration of infectiousness. The failed function j' at node i remains failed for an exponentially distributed time with mean 1/\eta_{j'}.

Expected secondary infections. During the failure period, each neighbor is exposed to a constant infection rate. The probability that a given neighbor is infected before the source recovers is

\frac{\rho}{\rho+\eta}.

For small \rho, this is approximately \rho/\eta. We use the exact linearization: the expected number of secondary infections per neighbor is

\frac{\rho}{\rho+\eta}

if the neighbor remains functional. Taking the limit as the initial number of failures tends to zero, the mean-field reproduction number is obtained by multiplying the infection rate by the mean duration 1/\eta. Hence the expected number of secondary failures of type j caused by a single failure of type j' is

M_{j,j'}(A)
=
\frac{1}{\eta_{j'}}
\left[
\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{node}}(1-\alpha_{j,j'})
+
c_{\text{in}}\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{in}}(1-\alpha_{j,j'})
+
c_{\text{out}}\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{out},0}(1-\alpha_{j,j'})(1-\Phi_j(A))
\right].

The mean-field reproduction number is

R_0(A)=\rho(M(A)).

Proposition D.2 (Monotonicity of R_0 in isolation)

If \Phi(A)\le \tilde\Phi(A) entrywise, then

R_0(\Phi(A))\ge R_0(\tilde\Phi(A)).

Proof.
Increasing \Phi_j(A) decreases the entries of M(A). By Perron–Frobenius theory, the spectral radius of a nonnegative matrix is nondecreasing in each entry. \square

Proposition D.3 (Subcriticality under isolation)

Suppose

R_0(0)>1

but the internal-only spectral radius satisfies

\rho\left(
\left[
\frac{1}{\eta_{j'}}
\left(
\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{node}}(1-\alpha_{j,j'})
+
c_{\text{in}}\rho_{j,j'}^{\text{in}}(1-\alpha_{j,j'})
\right)
\right]_{j,j'}
\right)
<
1.

Then there exists \Phi^*<1 such that

R_0(\Phi^*)<1.

Proof.
At full isolation \Phi_j=1, the external terms vanish. The remaining matrix is the internal-only matrix, whose spectral radius is less than 1. Since R_0 is continuous in \Phi, there exists \Phi^*<1 such that R_0(\Phi^*)<1. \square

D.4 Mean-Field Limit and Error Bound (Sketch)

We state a convergence result for the finite-node Markov process to the branching process described by M(A).

Theorem D.4 (Sparse random graph limit)

Assume the node set V_n is generated by a stochastic block model with m modules, intra-module edge probability p_{\text{in}}=c_{\text{in}}/n, and inter-module edge probability p_{\text{out}}=c_{\text{out}}/n, where c_{\text{in}},c_{\text{out}} are fixed. Let I_n(t) be the number of failed nodes of each function type. Then, as n\to\infty, the process I_n(t)/n converges in probability to the solution of the deterministic mean-field equations

\frac{d}{dt} y_j(t)
=
-\eta_j y_j(t)
+
(1-y_j(t))
\sum_{j'}
M_{j,j'}(A) y_{j'}(t),

where M(A) is the next-generation matrix.

Proof sketch.
This follows from standard convergence of Markov processes on sparse graphs to branching processes; see e.g. the references on contact processes on random graphs. The finite-population fluctuations are of order 1/\sqrt{n}. A rigorous proof requires coupling the original process with a branching process and using the fact that the graph is locally tree-like. We omit the full proof here and flag it as future work. \square

Proposition D.5 (Finite-sample error bound)

There exists a constant C>0 depending only on the model parameters such that for all t\in[0,T],

\mathbb{E}\left[
\left|
\frac{I_n(t)}{n}-y(t)
\right|
\right]
\le
\frac{C}{\sqrt{n}}.

Proof sketch.
This follows from the martingale representation of the Markov process and the boundedness of rates. The constant C can be estimated explicitly from the transition rates. \square

