Appendix F. Governance and Hierarchical Consensus

F.1 Delayed Consensus Protocol

Let L\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n} be the graph Laplacian of a connected network of governance agents. The delayed consensus dynamics are

\dot{x}(t)=-L x(t-\tau).

Lemma F.1

The consensus manifold is asymptotically stable if and only if

\tau\lambda_{\max}(L)<\frac{\pi}{2}.

Proof.
This is Theorem C.2. \square

F.2 Hierarchical Governance

Let the civilization be organized into L scale levels. At level l, the governance network has Laplacian L^{(l)} and round-trip delay \tau_{\text{loop}}^{(l)}. Define the governance latency index

\Gamma_l
=
\frac{2}{\pi}
\tau_{\text{loop}}^{(l)}
\lambda_{\max}(L^{(l)}).

Theorem F.2 (Governance viability hierarchy)

For each scale l, governance consensus is asymptotically stable only if

\Gamma_l<1.

If \Gamma_l\ge 1, then governance at scale l cannot be stabilized by continuous delayed consensus. In that case, either:

1. the decision authority must be devolved to scale l-1 (local autonomy), or
2. the decision must be pre-authorized, or
3. the communication topology must be redesigned to reduce \lambda_{\max}(L^{(l)}).

Proof.
The first statement is Lemma F.1 applied to L^{(l)}. The alternatives follow from the impossibility of continuous stabilization under the delay bound. \square

Definition F.3 — Governance local autonomy floor

For governance function G, define the minimum required local autonomy at level l as

a^{(l)}_{G,\min}
=
\begin{cases}
1, & \Gamma_l\ge 1,\\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

This makes the functional fractal property for governance a derived consequence of the latency theorem and the communication graph, not a stipulation.

